= Book Matter =

'''Book Matter''' is the semantic anatomy of a knowledge object: recognizable portions of a page that remain meaningful when read, retrieved, or transcluded independently.

Book Matter is not a domain, namespace, category tree, or entity type.

== Why it exists ==
A user or agent may need only one portion of a subject—for example its introduction, theory, mechanism, evidence, applications, or references. V2 therefore treats important sections as stable semantic interfaces without fragmenting every article into pseudo-files.

== Working vocabulary ==
Common Book Matter includes:

=== Orientation ===
* Introduction
* Definition
* Overview
* Background
* History

=== Explanation ===
* Theory
* Framework
* Mechanism
* Methods

=== Evidence ===
* Evidence
* Results
* Examples
* Case studies

=== Application and evaluation ===
* Applications
* Implementation
* Implications
* Limitations
* Challenges
* Criticism

=== Relations and sources ===
* Related concepts
* See also
* References
* Bibliography
* Further reading

The list is a controlled working vocabulary, not a requirement that every page contain every section. Page types may use specialized Book Matter where the content demands it.

== Transclusion principle ==
Book Matter should be addressable and reusable through MediaWiki transclusion mechanisms. The semantic section model is architectural; the exact extension or implementation may change as the MediaWiki stack evolves.

